{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "355",
    "case_summary": "A patient in their late 60s presented with a 4-day history of palpitations at rest and reported 2 months of exertional breathlessness. Medical history included metastatic bladder carcinoma followed by palliative chemotherapy, and pulmonary tuberculosis treated with antituberculous therapy for 6 months 10 years earlier.\n\nVital signs showed pulse rate of 84 beats per minute (bpm) and blood pressure of 112/76 mm Hg. Jugular venous pressure was notable for a prominent y descent. Cardiovascular examination was otherwise unremarkable.\n\n- Echocardiography: Showed biatrial dilatation, normal biventricular systolic function, grade III diastolic dysfunction, and significant transmitral flow variations.- Electrocardiogram (ECG):  First ECG:  Image Description: 12-lead ECG showing irregular narrow complex tachycardia with a ventricular rate of 78 bpm and atrial rate of 210 bpm. Inverted P-wave morphology in the inferior limb leads.    Post-cardioversion ECG:  Image Description: Shows prolonged P-wave duration (160 milliseconds). P waves were notched and negative in leads III and aVF and were isoelectric in lead II.",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Palpitations and Exertional Breathlessness with Prominent Y Descent and Diastolic Dysfunction",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.7,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "palpitations at rest",
        "exertional breathlessness",
        "prominent y descent",
        "biatrial dilatation",
        "grade III diastolic dysfunction"
      ],
      "excluded_candidates": [
        "Atrial Fibrillation: possible due to irregular narrow complex tachycardia, but other mechanisms also possible",
        "Cardiac Tamponade: less likely due to absence of pulsus paradoxus, but prominent y descent raises suspicion"
      ],
      "alarm_features": [
        "exertional breathlessness",
        "prominent y descent"
      ],
      "salient_findings": [
